    <!-- Detection Pattern Explanations -->
    <string name="explanation_stationary_user_moving_tower">This tower appears to be moving while you are stationary (%1$s). Real cell towers are fixed installations and should not move. This could indicate a mobile fake tower (IMSI catcher) that\'s following you or being transported nearby.</string>
    <string name="explanation_user_moving_tower_following">The tower appears to be moving with you as you travel (%1$s). While some tower handoffs are normal during travel, consistent movement matching your speed and direction could indicate a mobile surveillance device.</string>
    <string name="explanation_stationary_user_intermittent_tower">This tower keeps appearing and disappearing while you\'re stationary (%1$s). Real towers maintain consistent coverage. Intermittent connections suggest a portable device that\'s being turned on/off or moved in/out of range.</string>
    <string name="explanation_timing_advance_anomaly">Timing Advance values show unusual fluctuations while you\'re stationary (%1$s). TA should remain relatively constant when not moving. Significant changes could indicate signal manipulation or a fake tower adjusting its timing.</string>
    <string name="explanation_network_performance_anomaly">Network performance doesn\'t match signal strength (%1$s). Strong signals with poor performance could indicate a man-in-the-middle attack where the fake tower intercepts but doesn\'t properly relay your communications.</string>
    <string name="explanation_new_unknown_tower">This is a newly observed tower with limited historical data (%1$s). While new towers are deployed regularly, combined with other suspicious patterns, this could indicate a recently activated surveillance device.</string>
    <string name="explanation_unusual_signal_pattern">Signal strength shows abnormal fluctuations (%1$s). Real towers typically maintain more consistent signal levels. Erratic patterns could indicate equipment issues or intentional signal manipulation.</string>
    <string name="explanation_network_anomalies">General network irregularities detected (%1$s). This could include authentication failures, registration issues, or other network-layer anomalies that sometimes accompany fake tower operations.</string>
    <string name="explanation_registration_issues">Registration and authentication problems detected (%1$s). Fake towers may struggle to properly handle network registration procedures, leading to repeated connection attempts or authentication failures.</string>
    <string name="explanation_general_suspicion">Suspicious activity detected: %1$s (%2$s). This could indicate potential surveillance or network manipulation.</string>
    
    <!-- Severity and Confidence Levels -->
    <string name="multiple_detection_patterns">Multiple suspicious patterns detected:</string>
    
    <!-- Recommended Actions -->
    <string name="actions_high_accuracy">⚠️ HIGH RISK: Consider avoiding sensitive communications. Move to a different location. Monitor for additional suspicious activity.</string>
    <string name="actions_medium_accuracy">⚠️ MEDIUM RISK: Be cautious with sensitive communications. This could be a fake tower or network issue. Monitor the situation.</string>
    <string name="actions_low_accuracy">ℹ️ LOW RISK: This might be a network anomaly or new tower. Continue monitoring but no immediate action needed.</string>
    <string name="actions_very_low_accuracy">ℹ️ VERY LOW RISK: Minor anomaly detected. Likely normal network behavior but worth noting for future reference.</string>
    
    <!-- Educational Content -->
    <string name="educational_content_general">Fake towers (IMSI catchers) are surveillance devices that mimic real cell towers to intercept mobile communications. They can:\n\n• Track your location\n• Intercept calls and messages\n• Perform man-in-the-middle attacks\n• Collect subscriber identity information\n\nLegitimate reasons for detection patterns include:\n• New tower deployments\n• Network maintenance\n• Temporary coverage issues\n• Equipment malfunctions\n\nAlways consider context before taking action.</string>
    
